  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   We propose a multi-resolution harmonic analysis approach that leverages both local and global optimization. \n   The core idea is to optimize functions at varying resolutions, from coarse to fine, according to their structural properties. \n   This approach combines penalized optimization with Fourier-based rounding to ensure both feasibility and optimality.\n   Our reference implementation achieves C=0.380861, and we aim to push this further to 0.3808 or lower by systematically examining \n   mathematical structures that minimize correlations.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   We represent candidate solutions as piecewise constant functions on a grid, but optimize their forms using smoothing via \n   Fourier transforms. The objective is to minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx, subject to 0\u2264h(x)\u22641 and \u222bh=1 over [0,2].\n\n   Key mathematical components:\n   - Penalized optimization: We enforce constraints via gradient penalties\n   - Fourier rounding: Discretize and round continuous Fourier transforms\n   - Variable resolution: Use adaptive grid sizing (n\u2080=650, n\u2081=400, n\u2082=250) with transfer functions\n   - Lag correlation analysis: Focus optimization on the distribution of cross-correlation values\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   a) Initialize with provided parent or Fourier-based pseudo-random start\n   \n   b) Project to feasible space for soft constraints:\n        h\u0303 = h * (target_integral / \u222bh)\n        h_feas = clip(h\u0303, 0, 1)\n   \n   c) Main optimization loop:\n        Phase 1: High-resolution optimization (n=650)\n            - Use gradient descent with exponential smoothing (alpha_t = exp(-t))\n            - Compute gradient penalty: \u03c1 = 10^{-7} * ||\u2207\u222bh - desired\u222bh||\u00b2\n            - Progressive lag concentration: Focus on k positions with highest correlation\n            - Population model: Mutation probability p\u2098 = exp(-iter/500)\n            - Feasibility repair: Project is_infeasible individuals to random parents\n\n        Phase 2: Adaptive resolution switch (n=400)\n            - If improvement stalls over 15 iterations:\n                - Coarsen grid with interpolation\n                - Maintain correlation distribution and lags\n\n        Phase 3: Global Fourier rounding\n            - Analyze correlation hotspots to adjust spectral distribution\n            - Use Chebyshev coefficients for interpolation between resolutions\n\n        Phase 4: Final polishing with:\n            - Phase-dependent time budget allocation\n            - Early termination heuristic when no improvement in local gradients\n            - Recovery path through multi-resolution\n\n   d) Helper functions:\n        project_feasible(h: ndarray, target: float) -> ndarray\n        compute_correlation(h: ndarray) -> float\n        gradient_penalty(h: ndarray) -> ndarray\n        fea_to_fourier(h_feas: ndarray) -> ndarray\n        smooth_penalty(c: float, alpha: float) -> float\n        lag_distribution(h: ndarray) -> ndarray\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations:\n   Primary algorithm structure:\n     - Resolution: Start with n=650, auto-cooldown to n400 if stuck\n     - Stochastic optimization: L-BFGS-B with random restarts every 50 steps\n     - Correlation spectrum analysis: Focus on the 10% highest-correlation lags\n     - Budget-dependent scheduling: Adaptive step size (step_t = 0.001 * exp(-t/500))\n     - Differentiability handling: Smooth max replacements with temperature control\n\n   Variation 1: Higher resolution (n=2000) trials for final polishing\n   Variation 2: Add frequency-domain smoothing via spectral filters\n   Variation 3: Use ridge regression on lag space for correlation prediction\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   Time budget allocation:\n     - Resolution trials: 15% maximum\n     - Feasibility repair: 10% maximum\n     - Full cycle: 75% optimization\n     - Progress monitoring: Run 10-second checkpoints per estimate of time to target\n\n   Multi-resolution strategy:\n     - n\u2080=650 baseline (CPU-time: 10^6 operations)\n     - n\u2081=400 fallback (CPU-time: 1.2\u00d7 lower)\n     - n\u00b2=250 recovery (CPU-time: 1.7\u00d7 lower)\n\n   Fallback condition:\n     Detect stagnation when gradient norm < 1e-8 over 5 cycles or\n     when best improvement per generation < 1e-6 and optimization time > 500 seconds\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   Validation steps:\n     - Integral check: abs(sum(h)*dx - 1) < 1e-6\n     - Bounds check: all(0.0 <= h[i]) and all(h[i] <= 1.0) for all i\n     - Conflicting objective behavior: \n        Check against previous best if available\n        Re-evaluate with fallback method when suspect\n     - Lag regularization: Compute over 2n-1 lags for mode='full'\n\n   Final output format:\n     return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
